1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:03,318
This is Alt F four's Explainable Logistics Decision Intelligence submission.

2
00:00:03,318 --> 00:00:07,299
The business question is not just which model has the best score.

3
00:00:07,299 --> 00:00:11,280
It is which orders can be delivered, with the fleet actually available.

4
00:00:11,280 --> 00:00:14,597
Our verified solution serves seventy nine of eighty five orders.

5
00:00:14,597 --> 00:00:16,920
This walkthrough uses real executed notebook outputs.

6
00:00:16,920 --> 00:00:19,574
The narration is synthetic and requires team review.

7
00:00:21,074 --> 00:00:23,736
First, the notebook constructs waiting aware service labels.

8
00:00:23,736 --> 00:00:32,056
If a vehicle arrives at eight forty, the outlet opens at nine, and handling ends at nine twelve, service is twelve minutes, not thirty two.

9
00:00:32,056 --> 00:00:34,052
The twenty minute wait is excluded.

10
00:00:34,052 --> 00:00:38,046
Arrival after window close is late; arrival exactly at close is not.

11
00:00:38,046 --> 00:00:46,032
We join dispatched orders to their route legs one to one, exclude orders that never ran, and remove actual outcomes before building planned features.

12
00:00:47,532 --> 00:00:53,634
We compare eight explicit configurations for each delivery target, including boosted trees, extra trees, and linear baselines.

13
00:00:53,634 --> 00:00:57,224
Two earlier four week folds choose models and ensemble weights.

14
00:00:57,224 --> 00:01:01,890
The six week final holdout comes later, from January fourth to February fourteenth.

15
00:01:01,890 --> 00:01:04,402
Service prediction blends CatBoost and histogram boosting.

16
00:01:04,402 --> 00:01:08,709
Late probability blends X G Boost, CatBoost and Light G B M.

17
00:01:08,709 --> 00:01:13,017
The displayed results are from that frozen holdout, not the training data.

18
00:01:14,517 --> 00:01:21,452
Service mean absolute error is three point seven five minutes, and lateness area under the curve is zero point nine seven five.

19
00:01:21,452 --> 00:01:23,659
But the brand and depot table matters.

20
00:01:23,659 --> 00:01:24,920
Fresh dominates the sample.

21
00:01:24,920 --> 00:01:29,019
Tech has larger absolute handling errors, and its segment samples are much smaller.

22
00:01:29,019 --> 00:01:36,585
For operational review, one thousand and seventeen of the five thousand and fourteen test orders have predicted late probability of at least fifty percent.

23
00:01:36,585 --> 00:01:38,476
These are risks, not observed delays.

24
00:01:39,976 --> 00:01:45,479
Demand uses order date and includes every order, even deferred orders and orders that never ran.

25
00:01:45,479 --> 00:01:51,669
We build complete weekly series for each depot and brand, and forecast ten weeks from a fixed origin.

26
00:01:51,669 --> 00:01:55,796
Candidate models include rolling means, a seasonal baseline, ridge regression and boosting.

27
00:01:55,796 --> 00:02:03,705
The submitted network forecast peaks in week fifteen at about two thousand and seven cubic metres, including six hundred and eighty two chilled.

28
00:02:03,705 --> 00:02:06,113
This supports inventory and refrigerated availability planning.

29
00:02:07,613 --> 00:02:12,111
Overall total demand weighted absolute percentage error is six point zero eight percent.

30
00:02:12,111 --> 00:02:19,031
That hides Tech errors of twenty four point seven percent in Kandy and twenty nine point eight percent in Peliyagoda.

31
00:02:19,031 --> 00:02:21,107
Several series have negative R squared.

32
00:02:21,107 --> 00:02:24,913
We tested nine simple Tech baselines using only earlier chronological folds.

33
00:02:24,913 --> 00:02:30,795
The Kandy bias correction improved validation error by less than one percent; the Peliyagoda alternatives were worse.

34
00:02:30,795 --> 00:02:34,255
We kept the verified predictions and report these limitations openly.

35
00:02:35,755 --> 00:02:41,011
The peak day needs one hundred and eighty one point six cubic metres of chilled capacity.

36
00:02:41,011 --> 00:02:48,568
Four available refrigerated vehicles can carry at most one hundred and seventy two point four cubic metres across two full volume trips each.

37
00:02:48,568 --> 00:02:53,496
That is an optimistic bound: demand already exceeds it by nine point two cubic metres.

38
00:02:53,496 --> 00:03:00,066
C P Sat then enforces whole orders, depot, brand and district compatibility, van access, weight, volume and published time budgets.

39
00:03:00,066 --> 00:03:04,009
A separate maximum count solve proves the ceiling of seventy nine deliveries.

40
00:03:05,509 --> 00:03:09,281
We changed only the priority weights, leaving every hard constraint unchanged.

41
00:03:09,281 --> 00:03:16,139
Fresh first and balanced both serve seventy nine orders and deliver one hundred and forty point seven cubic metres chilled.

42
00:03:16,139 --> 00:03:21,283
Fairness first serves seventy seven, but clears all ten previously deferred orders instead of nine.

43
00:03:21,283 --> 00:03:26,770
That choice sacrifices two deliveries and fourteen point four six nine cubic metres of chilled volume.

44
00:03:26,770 --> 00:03:31,228
The comparison uses common reference points, because different policy scores have different scales.

45
00:03:31,228 --> 00:03:34,657
All three solutions are optimal and pass the original checker.

46
00:03:36,157 --> 00:03:40,896
The final notebook cell reloads the saved models and reproduces both prediction files.

47
00:03:40,896 --> 00:03:44,177
Template checks preserve every identifier, column and row order.

48
00:03:44,177 --> 00:03:48,551
The allocation checker passes, and all three original submission files remain unchanged.

49
00:03:48,551 --> 00:03:54,019
These task horizons are distinct; they are not one jointly solved real world route plan.

50
00:03:54,019 --> 00:03:59,852
Optimality covers the published competition constraints, not unmodelled traffic, detailed arrival windows or remaining weekly fuel.

51
00:03:59,852 --> 00:04:04,226
The package discloses the A I assistance and requires human team review.

52
00:04:04,226 --> 00:04:08,600
Our contribution is a decision that can be explained, checked and defended.
